<section id="editors" class="flex flex-col gap-4 scroll-mt-4 border-t-2 border-eist pt-8">
  <div class="flex flex-col gap-1">
    <p class="eyebrow text-eist">Only editors see this part</p>
    <h2 class="font-display text-2xl">For editors</h2>
  </div>
  <p>
    Editors keep the course right: the lessons themselves, which real recordings learners hear, and the AI replies learners have questioned.
    Everything below is on pages learners can't open. Every change to a lesson is recorded, so nothing you do there is final.
  </p>

  <h3 class="font-semibold text-lg mt-2">Editing a lesson</h3>
  <ul class="list-disc pl-5 flex flex-col gap-1">
    <li><strong>Edit</strong> is on each lesson, and beside each lesson on the Lessons page.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Lesson details</strong> has the title and the word list: each word's Irish, English, gender, plural and pronunciation.
      The quiz is built from the word list, so changes there reach learners' reviews.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Intro video</strong> takes a YouTube or Vimeo link, an embed code, or a link to an .mp4 file, with an optional transcript.</li>
    <li><strong>Rich content</strong> holds free text, videos, images and PDFs, in any order. <strong>Lesson content</strong> is the grammar from the book.</li>
    <li><strong>AI tutor settings</strong> tell the Practise chat what to focus on for this lesson. Leave it empty for the general default.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Published</strong> decides whether learners can see the lesson. Drafts show a Draft label and only editors see them.
      <strong>Mark as reviewed</strong> records that someone has checked a draft; unchecked drafts show <strong>Needs review</strong> on the Lessons page.
    </li>
    <li><strong>View as learner</strong> shows the lesson without the editing buttons.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>History</strong> lists every change to the lesson, newest first, and who made it.
      <strong>Undo</strong> puts back what a change replaced, and the undo is recorded too.
    </li>
    <li><strong>New lesson</strong> is at the top of the Lessons page.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3 class="font-semibold text-lg mt-2">Recordings for words</h3>
  <p>
    <strong>Abairt recordings</strong>, on each lesson, lists the words that have no recording of their own and finds Mayo recordings of them on Abairt, a word at a time as you scroll.
    Each suggestion has been run through ABAIR's speech recogniser: <em>checked</em> means it heard the word, otherwise it shows what it heard instead.
    Listen before approving. Learners hear a recording as soon as it's approved, credited to the speaker, and approving one sets that word's other suggestions aside.
    <strong>Approve checked recordings</strong> approves the best checked one for every word at once; listen to a few first.
  </p>

  <h3 class="font-semibold text-lg mt-2">Listening clips</h3>
  <p>
    <strong>Listening clips</strong>, on each lesson, shows the short phrases from Abairt that learners get once they've finished the lesson: up to two per word,
    3 to 12 words long, kept only when the recogniser heard the word and most of the phrase. They're found automatically.
  </p>
  <p>
    Nothing needs approving here, but please look through them. <strong>Hide from learners</strong> any clip with a wrong or misleading translation,
    audio that doesn't match the words, or anything that isn't suitable. You can also hide a clip from its own page, and <strong>Show to learners again</strong> undoes it.
    A hidden clip's whole conversation is no longer offered unless another clip leads to it.
  </p>

  <h3 class="font-semibold text-lg mt-2">Flagged conversation replies</h3>
  <p>
    When a learner flags an AI reply in a conversation, a <strong>flagged replies</strong> button appears at the top of the Lessons page.
    Each flag shows what the learner said and the reply, and whether ABAIR or OpenAI wrote it.
    Check the Irish, then <strong>Dismiss</strong> it. If the reply really was wrong, note what was wrong and pass it on to Seosamh:
    replies can't be edited, but a pattern of mistakes tells us what to change in how the conversation is set up.
  </p>

  <h3 class="font-semibold text-lg mt-2">Other things to know</h3>
  <ul class="list-disc pl-5 flex flex-col gap-1">
    <li>New sign-ups wait for approval before they can use the course. Seosamh approves them for now.</li>
    <li>Search finds draft lessons too, for editors.</li>
    <li>
      The course can also be edited through Claude, using a connector that can read and change lessons, words and grammar but can't delete anything.
      Changes made that way appear in each lesson's History like any other. Ask Seosamh if you'd like it set up.
    </li>
    <li>
      The course doesn't use AI to write its Irish, and that includes the Claude connector: use it for structure, English and tidying, not for writing Irish.
      Lesson Irish should come from the book, from speakers, or from you.
    </li>
  </ul>
</section>
